WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.619
This lesson covers clogged code in automated CI-CD pipelines.

2
00:00:04.619 --> 00:00:08.239
We'll look at why non-interactive mode is required,

3
00:00:08.239 --> 00:00:10.659
how the dash p flag enables it,

4
00:00:10.659 --> 00:00:13.199
how to structure tasks for automation,

5
00:00:13.199 --> 00:00:15.500
GitHub Actions integration patterns,

6
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.739
and the security considerations that

7
00:00:17.739 --> 00:00:20.139
matter most in automated contexts.

8
00:00:20.139 --> 00:00:24.819
Let's get into it. A hung CI job doesn't fail loudly.

9
00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:27.180
It sits there consuming runner minutes

10
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.260
until a global timeout kills it.

11
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.340
Nearly every case comes down to one missing flag.

12
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.220
The dash p flag, also called print mode,

13
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.939
switches clogged code to non-interactive operation.

14
00:00:38.939 --> 00:00:42.540
Pass it and clogged code takes the prompt you give it,

15
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.860
runs the task, prints the result to

16
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.540
standard output, and exits cleanly.

17
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.299
No interactive prompt, no waiting for more input.

18
00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:55.700
Non-interactive mode is the execution mode that dash p

19
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.939
activates where the agent works as

20
00:00:57.939 --> 00:01:00.419
a one-shot tool that takes a task,

21
00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:02.380
completes it, and exits.

22
00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:07.220
A pipeline job hang is what you get when dash p is missing.

23
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.639
Clogged code waits for interactive input the runner can

24
00:01:10.639 --> 00:01:14.300
never provide and the job blocks until it times out.

25
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.660
Let's talk about why dash p is

26
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.680
non-negotiable in automated pipelines.

27
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.739
When a CI-CD runner launches a process,

28
00:01:22.739 --> 00:01:26.220
there's no human present to answer interactive prompts.

29
00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.300
Clogged code's default mode is interactive.

30
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.900
It opens a session and waits.

31
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.239
In a runner environment with no terminal,

32
00:01:34.239 --> 00:01:36.220
that wait never ends.

33
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.239
The job hangs, consumes runner minutes,

34
00:01:39.239 --> 00:01:41.160
and eventually times out.

35
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.160
The dash p flag changes this completely.

36
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.220
It tells clogged code to run

37
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.379
its full agentic loop non-interactively.

38
00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:51.779
It reads the prompt, does the work,

39
00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:55.419
calling tools across as many turns as the task needs,

40
00:01:55.419 --> 00:01:58.639
then writes output to standard output and exits

41
00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:01.739
with a status code the pipeline can act on.

42
00:02:01.739 --> 00:02:04.220
The loop still runs to completion.

43
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.040
It just never stops to ask a human for input.

44
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.860
That's the contract automation needs.

45
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.440
In practice, you rarely pass dash p alone.

46
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.100
It comes with a small set of

47
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.800
companion flags that make a headless run predictable.

48
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.139
Output format is the big one.

49
00:02:21.139 --> 00:02:23.899
Pass output format, JSON,

50
00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:26.860
and clogged code returns a structured result.

51
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.100
The next pipeline step can parse

52
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.419
instead of scraping free text.

53
00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:33.100
There's also a streaming variant,

54
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.000
stream JSON, that emits

55
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.020
events as they happen if you want live progress.

56
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.100
Allowed tools lets you scope

57
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.279
exactly which tools the run is permitted to invoke,

58
00:02:43.279 --> 00:02:46.619
which matters a lot when no human is watching.

59
00:02:46.619 --> 00:02:49.100
If a job should build on an earlier session,

60
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:50.660
rather than start clean,

61
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.020
resume picks up a specific past session

62
00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.740
and continue picks up the most recent one.

63
00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.380
Underneath all of them, the contract stays the same.

64
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.360
Output goes to standard output.

65
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.779
Exit code zero means success,

66
00:03:04.779 --> 00:03:07.979
and any non-zero code means something went wrong.

67
00:03:08.979 --> 00:03:12.740
Structuring tasks well is what makes automation reliable.

68
00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.779
Four principles matter here.

69
00:03:14.779 --> 00:03:17.619
First, keep each invocation atomic.

70
00:03:17.619 --> 00:03:19.800
One task, one outcome.

71
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.919
Don't ask clogged code to lint a file

72
00:03:21.919 --> 00:03:25.039
and update documentation in the same call.

73
00:03:25.039 --> 00:03:28.639
Second, write prompts that are fully self-contained.

74
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:31.940
The agent has no memory of previous pipeline runs

75
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.699
unless you explicitly include that context in the prompt.

76
00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:39.240
Third, request structured output, usually JSON,

77
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.880
so the next pipeline step can parse the result

78
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.800
without brittle text matching.

79
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.399
Fourth, design tasks to be idempotent.

80
00:03:47.399 --> 00:03:49.320
Pipelines retry on failure,

81
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.639
so if the same task runs twice,

82
00:03:51.639 --> 00:03:53.679
the result should be the same both times.

83
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.960
Interactive mode and non-interactive mode

84
00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.119
are worth comparing directly.

85
00:03:59.119 --> 00:04:01.399
Interactive mode is the default.

86
00:04:01.399 --> 00:04:03.479
Clogged code opens a REPL session

87
00:04:03.479 --> 00:04:06.479
and carries on a multi-turn conversation with you.

88
00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.800
It's designed for developer workstations,

89
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.660
where a human is present and engaged throughout the session.

90
00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:15.539
You can ask follow-up questions, refine tasks,

91
00:04:15.539 --> 00:04:17.640
and iterate in real time.

92
00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.200
Non-interactive mode, activated by dash P, is the opposite.

93
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.660
Clogged code accepts one prompt, does the work, and exits.

94
00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:29.000
There's no session, no conversation,

95
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.739
no waiting for more input.

96
00:04:30.739 --> 00:04:33.000
These two modes are mutually exclusive

97
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.920
for a given invocation.

98
00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:38.160
Any scenario describing automation, scheduled jobs,

99
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.480
or CI-CD integration calls for non-interactive mode

100
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:43.859
and the dash P flag.

101
00:04:44.899 --> 00:04:46.820
For GitHub Actions specifically,

102
00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.540
there's an official integration,

103
00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.899
and it's the path you should reach for.

104
00:04:50.899 --> 00:04:53.339
It's the Clogged Code Action workflow,

105
00:04:53.339 --> 00:04:57.059
and you set it up by running the install GitHub app command,

106
00:04:57.059 --> 00:05:01.059
which wires up the action and the GitHub app for you.

107
00:05:01.059 --> 00:05:03.619
Once it's in place, you trigger Clogged Code

108
00:05:03.619 --> 00:05:07.140
by mentioning at Clogged in an issue or pull request,

109
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.899
or by passing a prompt directly to the action.

110
00:05:09.899 --> 00:05:12.820
You still store your Anthropic API key

111
00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.100
as a GitHub Actions secret,

112
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.100
so it never lands in your workflow file.

113
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.260
Now, you can run a raw Clogged dash P command

114
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:22.980
inside an action step,

115
00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:28.019
but that's really just general agent SDK automation running in CI,

116
00:05:28.019 --> 00:05:31.820
not the documented GitHub Actions integration.

117
00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:35.839
When a scenario points specifically at GitHub Actions,

118
00:05:35.839 --> 00:05:37.500
reach for the official action.

119
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.140
In automated contexts,

120
00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.820
permission scoping is a security requirement,

121
00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:44.739
not just a best practice.

122
00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.940
The principle is minimum necessary permissions.

123
00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.279
If your task only needs to read files,

124
00:05:50.279 --> 00:05:52.200
don't grant write access.

125
00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.459
If the task is purely file-based,

126
00:05:54.459 --> 00:05:57.040
restrict or disable network tools.

127
00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:00.160
If Clogged Code only needs to touch one directory,

128
00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.059
scope it to that directory.

129
00:06:02.059 --> 00:06:04.100
Why does this matter in automation?

130
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.579
Because pipelines run unattended.

131
00:06:06.579 --> 00:06:08.899
If a task behaves unexpectedly

132
00:06:08.899 --> 00:06:11.899
due to a bad prompt or unexpected input,

133
00:06:11.899 --> 00:06:15.220
narrow permissions limit how much damage can happen.

134
00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.519
Broad permissions in an automated context

135
00:06:17.519 --> 00:06:19.320
are a risk you don't need to take.

136
00:06:20.299 --> 00:06:23.299
Let's talk about output parsing and error handling,

137
00:06:23.299 --> 00:06:26.519
because these are where automation breaks down in practice.

138
00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.920
Capture Clogged Code's standard output

139
00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.019
into a shell variable or a file immediately.

140
00:06:32.019 --> 00:06:34.899
Don't assume it will always return structured data.

141
00:06:34.899 --> 00:06:36.100
If something goes wrong,

142
00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.619
the agent may return a plain error message.

143
00:06:38.619 --> 00:06:41.660
Check the exit code after every invocation.

144
00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.940
A non-zero exit should fail the pipeline step.

145
00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.579
If you requested JSON,

146
00:06:46.579 --> 00:06:49.859
validate its shape before using it downstream,

147
00:06:49.859 --> 00:06:53.579
and always set a timeout on Clogged Code pipeline steps.

148
00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.859
A task that stalls for any reason

149
00:06:55.859 --> 00:06:57.859
will hold up your entire pipeline

150
00:06:57.859 --> 00:07:00.040
until the runner's global timeout fires,

151
00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:02.200
which is usually much longer than you want.

152
00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:07.420
Automation asks you to think about Clogged Code differently.

153
00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:09.220
It isn't something you talk to.

154
00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:13.140
It's a step in a pipeline that either finishes or fails.

155
00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:17.040
That one shift is what makes headless runs work,

156
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:20.339
because a step that can pause to ask a question

157
00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:23.100
is a step that'll sit there burning runner minutes

158
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.220
until a timeout kills it.

159
00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.779
Scoping, parsing, and timeouts

160
00:07:27.779 --> 00:07:31.040
all follow from taking that seriously.

161
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.640
That closes out this lesson.

162
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.100
I'll see you in the next one.

